{# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   customer_response — v1.1

   The customer-facing reply (SRS Steps 32-33).

   Design notes for whoever edits this next:

   * The inputs are the RECONCILED record and the retrieved policy — never
     raw Pipeline 1 output. A classification the rules overrode must not
     reach a customer through the back door of the reply.
   * What may and may not be promised is stated explicitly, derived from
     Pipeline 2's eligibility findings. The response guard enforces the same
     rule deterministically afterwards; saying it here means the guard
     usually has nothing to do, which is cheaper than regenerating.
   * Only ELIGIBLE authorises a commitment. CONDITIONAL and
     REQUIRES_VERIFICATION are shown to the model as things it must describe
     as pending, because that is exactly the SRS 1.8 #9 trap.
   * The reply is for the customer. Rule references, internal codes and
     department names do not belong in it.
   * v1.1: optional fields of the reconciled record are read with .get().
     Under v1.0 they were read as attributes, and StrictUndefined -- which is
     there so a missing policy_context can never render as a silent blank --
     raised on any caller that omitted primary_issue. Required variables are
     still read as attributes, so that protection is unchanged.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
